title: Back to zero
mode: draw
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
parts: dff and not chip:my_xor
needs: my_xor
---- intro
A counter needs a way back to 0. Here, when `rst` is on at a tick, both bits become 0, whatever they were. When `rst` is off, it counts as usual.

Each flip-flop gets its usual next value, let through only while `rst` is **off**: an AND with NOT `rst`, the "letting a signal through" trick.
---- task
Make `q1 q0` count up one step per tick, and go back to 0 at a tick when `rst` is on.
---- start
input rst 0 0
clock clk 0 280
led q0 620 40
led q1 620 200
---- reference
input rst
clock clk
led q0, q1
wire a[1], b[1]
a = dff(~a & ~rst, clk)
b = dff((b ^ a) & ~rst, clk)
q0 = a
q1 = b
---- answer
input rst 0 0
clock clk 0 280
not nr 120 0
not n0 160 100
and g0 300 40
dff f0 440 40
chip x1 my_xor 240 200
and g1 360 200
dff f1 480 200
led q0 620 40
led q1 620 200
rst -> nr.in
f0.q -> n0.in
n0 -> g0.0
nr -> g0.1
g0 -> f0.d
clk -> f0.clk
f0.q -> q0
f1.q -> x1.a
f0.q -> x1.b
x1.y -> g1.0
nr -> g1.1
g1 -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q1
---- hint1
Start from your counter: NOT `q0` feeds bit 0, `q1` XOR `q0` feeds bit 1. Now each of those goes through an AND before the flip-flop.
---- hint2
Make NOT `rst` once. AND it with NOT `q0` for the first flip-flop's `d`, and with the XOR for the second one's.
---- solution
The counter's next values, NOT `q0` and `q1` XOR `q0`, each go through an AND with NOT `rst` on the way to `d`. With `rst` on, both ANDs give 0.
---- done
This is a **synchronous** reset: it waits for the clock, like everything else in the circuit. Almost every flip-flop in a real design has one.
